Note, on line 1903 (later in this function) we call the `transformForRuntime()` method which sets envvars for the main container -- so there are still certain envvars that a user could never customize for this container.

This brings up a general question -- what is the "optimal" way to *always* ensure that user-supplied envvars take precedence over the variables that we set in these transform functions? How do we future-proof this so we don't accidentally add code to one of these transform functions that invalidates the order of precedence?

Without putting much thought into this, one option might be to add a defer statement in each `Transform*` function where user-supplied envvars are processed. This would ensure user-supplied envvars are always added last after all other transformations are applied. For example:
```
func TransformToolkit(. . .) error {
  defer func() {
	if len(config.Toolkit.Env) > 0 {
		for _, env := range config.Toolkit.Env {
			setContainerEnv(&(obj.Spec.Template.Spec.Containers[0]), env.Name, env.Value)
		}
	}
  }
  . . .
}
```
