This test mutates the global upgrade driver name via upgrade.SetDriverName("gpu") but doesn't restore it afterwards. Because DriverName is a package-level global in k8s-operator-libs, this can leak into other tests in the same process and cause order-dependent failures. Consider saving the previous value and restoring it with t.Cleanup()/defer.
```suggestion
func TestClearUpgradeLabelsWhereDriverNotRunning(t *testing.T) {
	// Preserve and restore the global driver name to avoid leaking state into other tests.
	previousDriverName := upgrade.DriverName
	t.Cleanup(func() {
		upgrade.SetDriverName(previousDriverName)
	})
```

The test hard-codes the upgrade label key ("nvidia.com/gpu-driver-upgrade-state") even though production code uses upgrade.GetUpgradeStateLabelKey(). This makes the test brittle if the label key format changes in k8s-operator-libs or if the driver name changes. Prefer deriving the label key from upgrade.GetUpgradeStateLabelKey() (after setting the driver name) instead of duplicating the string constant.
